{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790343600,"nonce":"zh5e4k2vo0KfZF9Oiuk6K6s5C7Y=","title":"Lua: a value-called builtin reports a missing later argument before a bad earlier one","message":"The value wrappers (library/stdlib.rs, the BUILTINS loop) check every required parameter for presence before any argument's type, so a builtin called through a value with a wrong first argument and too few arguments names the wrong one. Reproducer: local g = string.rep; print(pcall(function() return g({}) end)). lua5.4: 'bad argument #1 to 'g' (string expected, got table)'. zylua (both tiers): 'bad argument #2 to 'string.rep' (number expected, got no value)'. The reference checks arguments in order (luaL_checklstring(1) then luaL_checkinteger(2)). The name is a separate difference: the reference names the function by the call site's variable ('g') where it has one; see 0903215 for the qualified-name side. Found while working LO-error-messages.","files":null}]}